   "title": "Control-side routing: how the thermistors and the BTRY CHG LED are wired (read from the sheet)",
   "text": "732A-1003 is hand-drawn with a dozen parallel buses between the Schmitt trigger, Q5 and P3; they were followed one by one at 600 dpi and the result is the dataset's reading, not a statement in the manual. R11 hangs from the TP2 line (the CR26 / CR14 node) down to Q5's base, and CR22 (1 mA) carries that node's current into Q3's base / Q4's collector. P3-11 is the TP2 line and P3-12 the Q5 base node, so A6 RT1 (THERMISTOR) is in parallel with R11; P3-15 is the top of R12 (the CR14 anode, also Q5's emitter) and P3-16 the R12 / R13 tap, so A6 RT2 (THERMISTOR VOLTAGE COMPENSATION) is in parallel with R12. P3-13 (+ BATTERY CHARGE LED) is the DS1-cathode / Q2-base node and P3-14 (-) is Q3's collector node, so the front-panel BTRY CHG LED (A1 DS2) sits in parallel with R6 and lights whenever DS1 does. Q4's emitter goes straight to the shared emitter node above R10 / CR15; R8 is in series with CR11 from Q3's base to that node, not in Q4's emitter. Nothing in Table 4-3 or §4-44 depends on these details, but they explain why a cold battery (A6 RT1 high) or an open thermistor lead stops the high-rate charge, and why a BTRY CHG indicator that never lights with DS1 lit points at the W2 harness or A1 DS2 rather than at A3.",
